artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $\mathbb{H}$ denote the skew field of real quaternions, and let $A \in \mathbb{H}^{n \times n}$ be a constant quaternion matrix. Consider the recursion
\[ P_0 = I_n, \qquad P_k = A P_{k-1} - P_{k-1}\, \alpha_k \quad (k \ge 1), \]
where $\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \ldots, \alpha_m \in \mathbb{H}$ are quaternion constants to be selected. Putzer's construction of a fundamental solution matrix for the homogeneous linear quaternion dynamic equation $x^{\Delta}(t) = A\, x(t)$ on a time scale requires the existence of a finite $m$ and a choice of constants $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_m$ for which $P_m = 0$.

Develop a theory and algorithm for the following selection problem: characterize, for an arbitrary $A \in \mathbb{H}^{n\times n}$, when and how the quaternion constants $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_m$ can be chosen so that the above recursion terminates with $P_m = 0$. The procedure must apply in particular to quaternion matrices that fail to possess $n$ right linearly independent eigenvectors. Specifically, provide:
\begin{enumerate}
\item necessary and sufficient conditions on $A$ guaranteeing the existence of such constants $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_m$ and a (sharp) bound on the minimal such $m$;
\item a constructive algorithm that, given $A$, outputs admissible quaternion constants $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_m$ realizing $P_m = 0$;
\item a uniqueness/equivalence description of the set of admissible selections, taking into account non-commutativity of $\mathbb{H}$ (in particular the behavior under similarity $\alpha \mapsto q^{-1}\alpha q$).
\end{enumerate}

